  "prediction": "**Executive Summary**\n\nThe report examines the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA), a statute that grants the President extensive authority to regulate economic transactions in response to national emergencies. Since its enactment in 1977, IEEPA has been used by Presidents to impose sanctions on foreign governments, entities, and individuals, often with little congressional oversight.\n\nThe report highlights the evolution of IEEPA, from its initial intent to restrain presidential power to its current use as a tool for imposing economic sanctions. The statute has been invoked 54 times since 1977, with an average of nearly one declaration of national emergency per year. The longest-running national emergency, declared in 1979, has been in effect for over 40 years.\n\nThe report also examines the criticisms of IEEPA, including its lack of definition of key terms such as \"national emergency\" and \"unusual and extraordinary threat.\" Scholars have argued that the statute has become a grant of legislative power to the President, allowing for the imposition of sanctions without adequate congressional review.\n\nThe report concludes by highlighting several areas for congressional consideration, including the need to define key terms, establish clear procedures for terminating national emergencies, and provide for greater congressional oversight of presidential actions under IEEPA. The report also notes that Congress has the authority to amend IEEPA and the National Emergencies Act (NEA) to provide for greater accountability and transparency in the use of emergency powers.\n\n**Key Findings**\n\n* IEEPA has been invoked 54 times since 1977, with an average of nearly one declaration of national emergency per year.\n* The longest-running national emergency, declared in 1979, has been in effect for over 40 years.\n* IEEPA has been used to impose sanctions on foreign governments, entities, and individuals, often with little congressional oversight.\n* The statute lacks clear definitions of key terms, including \"national emergency\" and \"unusual and extraordinary threat.\"\n* Scholars have argued that IEEPA has become a grant of legislative power to the President, allowing for the imposition of sanctions without adequate congressional review.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\n* Define key terms, including \"national emergency\" and \"unusual and extraordinary threat.\"\n* Establish clear procedures for terminating national emergencies.\n* Provide for greater congressional oversight of presidential actions under IEEPA.\n* Amend IEEPA and the NEA to provide for greater accountability and transparency in the use of emergency powers.",
